From fb2dfb0b6c333a17f94d8d5851513d5cc490d950 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jjude80 <18168364+jjude80@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 00:34:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix(access): revoke the previous role's grants on
 demotion

updateMember seeded the new role's defaults on a role change but never withdrew
the old role's. Role defaults are materialized as ordinary grant rows, so a
demoted member kept them -- users:manage_permissions among them, which is a
self-escalation primitive: the demoted member can PATCH their own permissions
and grant back exactly what the demotion removed.

pruneSupersededRoleDefaultGrants deletes the previous role's defaults that the
new role does not confer, only where granted_by_user_id IS NULL, and runs inside
the membership transaction. A permission a human granted deliberately carries
that human's id and survives the demotion.

The NULL predicate is only sound if role defaults never record a granting human,
and three paths did: board-claim, company-create and company-clone all stamped
their owner's id onto owner defaults. Those rows then looked like explicit grants
and a later demotion could not withdraw them, so the escalation path stayed open
for exactly the highest-privilege principals. Reproduced on this branch's parent
before the change: a board-claim owner demoted through updateMember kept both
joins:approve and users:manage_permissions. ensureHumanRoleDefaultGrants and
ensureRoleDefaultGrants no longer accept a granting human, so role defaults are
recorded as role defaults and the mistake is a compile error.

Both helpers take Pick<Db, "insert" | "select" | "delete">, so the transaction
handle is passed at the updateMember call site without `tx as unknown as Db`.

This also makes the two role-change writers agree. updateMemberAndPermissions
already replaced the whole set; updateMember now withdraws what the old role
contributed. Same demotion, same grants, whichever endpoint the caller used.

Rows written before this change stay ambiguous. A row that is both a role default
and explicitly held is undecidable from the table alone, because
principal_permission_grants has no provenance column. Those rows keep current
behaviour and need a backfill policy decision.

Contract change, called out as a risk: for a human principal,
PATCH /companies/:id/members/:id/permissions goes from exact-set to
union-with-role-defaults, so an owner can no longer revoke a permission the role
implies and changing the role is the only lever. Agent principals keep exact-set
semantics.

Verification (run here): 31/31 in access-service.test.ts and
access-routes-permissions-upgrade.test.ts; 228/228 across the access,
board-claim, company-portability, cloud-floor and provisioning suites; tsc
--noEmit clean. With the service changes stashed, all three demotion tests fail
and 23 pre-existing assertions still pass.

